  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/horabagrus-nigricollaris",
      "html": "<p>The black collared catfish, Horabagrus nigricollaris, is a bagrid catfish endemic to the Chalakudy River in Kerala, south-western India. The rarer of the two species in the genus in the hobby, it resembles the much larger H. brachysoma. Both are usually sold as juveniles, and in this species the black shoulder marking extends up and over the top of the body, giving the collar that names it.</p><ul><li>Needs strong current, with the filter turning the tank over 8–12 times an hour</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/horabagrus-nigricollaris",
      "html": "<p>Endemic to the Chalakudy River in the state of Kerala, south-western India.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/horabagrus-nigricollaris",
      "html": "<p>Apparently, it lives in the stream-like headwaters of the Chalakudy drainage.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/horabagrus-nigricollaris",
      "html": "<p>Standard length: 30 cm SL. Aquarium base: 120 × 45 cm. Volume: ~243 litres.</p><p>Unsuitable for tanks smaller than 120 × 45 × 45 cm (~243 litres).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/horabagrus-nigricollaris",
      "html": "<p>It's a nocturnal species, so provide dim lighting and refuges in the form of big chunks of bogwood, tangles of beech branches, large rocks or lengths of plastic piping of a suitable bore. Ensure that any decor is either secured or is too heavy to be moved around by the fish. A large and efficient filter is needed to cope with the amounts of biological waste produced by a fish of this size. Given the fact it originates from hill streams, a good water flow with a high oxygen content would seem appropriate.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/horabagrus-nigricollaris",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 23–25°C</li><li>pH: 5.5–7.5</li><li>Hardness: 5–25 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/horabagrus-nigricollaris",
      "html": "<p>Most specimens are easy to feed, accepting a wide range of dried and meaty frozen foods. Feed a mixture of dried pellets as well as frozen prawns, mussels, earthworms etc. You may need to add food at lights out to ensure it gets its share, as it can be very secretive.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/horabagrus-nigricollaris",
      "html": "<p>Peaceful for its size and does well in a community of other placid, similarly-sized fish. It will eat any tankmate it can fit in its mouth, so be warned. Decent choices include larger cyprininds and characins, other catfish, cichlids and the smaller species of Polypterus. It will not squabble with conspecifics and can be kept singly or as a small group.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/horabagrus-nigricollaris",
      "html": "<p>Unknown.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/horabagrus-nigricollaris",
      "html": "<p>Unrecorded in aquaria.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/horabagrus-nigricollaris",
      "html": "<p>There are only 2 species currently in the genus Horabagrus, with this one being the rarer in the hobby. H. brachysoma is the other, and it resembles H. nigricollaris quite closely. It is a much larger fish and is unsuitable for most home aquaria. Confusion can arise as both are usually sold as juveniles, at around 2-3\" in length. They can be told apart by examining the black shoulder markings found on either side of the fish. In H. brachysoma this is no more than a vaguely circular dark blotch. In H. nigricollaris the marking extends up and over the top of the body. This gives the impression the fish is wearing a dark collar, and gave rise to the species name.</p>"
    }
  ],
